# The Devices tab, and what a device says about itself.
#
# The state used to be a word on the supporting line - "connected", "paired", "needs pairing" -
# in the dim ink every supporting line takes, which meant a list of radios read as three
# identical rows until each one had been read. It is a capsule now, in the family that says
# what the state *is*: the good tone for the one we are on, warning for the one that will fail
# on StartNotify until it is bonded, the quiet accent for a radio we merely know about.
#
# The two facts swapped lines to make room, and that is the half worth watching for: how well
# we hear a radio drops to the supporting line, because how a radio is attached is a detail and
# whether it is the one we are on is not.

scene demo
tab radio
key a
hold 1200

# The first row is the USB port, which is where the client puts it, and this one is a node in
# its UF2 bootloader. It is the only refusal a USB row can carry: the cable is fine and the
# board is healthy, but what is on the other end speaks no protobuf, so A will not open a link
# and the row says why instead of the list quietly dropping it. Warning rather than error for
# the same reason "needs pairing" is - there is something the user can do, and it is one press
# of reset.
key down
hold 900

# Down the list. The cursor's fill is the other pairing the capsule has to survive - a badge is
# a stated family either way, so it does not restate itself under the cursor.
key down
hold 900
key down
hold 900

# Armed to be forgotten. The row goes bad in every part of itself, the capsule included: a red
# row carrying a quiet accent pill would be two statements where the press is asking one.
key y
hold 1400
key b
hold 900

# The last row is the one that is not a radio: the network address, and the only way on the
# device to type one. It draws in the same three slots as everything above it, so that when a
# network link comes up and discovery publishes a real row in its place, nothing on the panel
# moves. A here connects; Y opens the keyboard on the address.
key down
key down
hold 1400
key y
hold 1600

# Out through the keyboard's own cancel rather than with B, which is backspace here and would
# eat the address one character at a time. Up from the top row wraps onto the action row and
# Left from its first key wraps onto the last, which is cancel.
key up
key left
key a
hold 900

# The same list in every theme. The capsule's fill and its ink come from one call, so nothing
# here is a pair anybody matched by eye.
key up
key up
key up
key up
key up
theme light
hold 1000
theme contrast
hold 1000
theme colorblind
hold 1400
